The `@pancake/web` package compiles pancake's protocol core to WebAssembly. A
browser uses the same `Session` operations as the native TypeScript binding;
JavaScript supplies the byte channel and scheduling.

## Dial

Your page is the dialing half of a conversation, so this walkthrough assumes
the other half already exists: a service of yours listening on the keyed TCP
transport in a managed binding. Starting it hands you its
`ocapn://` locator, one pasteable string carrying the service's public key and
socket address. One wrinkle before the code: that listener speaks raw TCP and
a page can only open WebSockets, so the dial reaches it through a
[relay]($link.page('relay')). Raw `keyed-relay` reaches the peer by key but
leaves CapTP frames in the clear, so the relay can read them and nothing proves
who answered. The browser can install the explicit keyed-relay connector when
that route is intended.

Load the wasm module to use the `@pancake/runtime` API. The built-in browser
runtime opens `tcp` and `keyed-tcp` through WebSocket, and can add iroh,
keyed-relay, or WebRTC connectors explicitly. The locator transport determines
how the bytes travel.

```=html
<!-- site-examples:dial:browser GENERATED by `zig build gen-site-examples`; edits are overwritten -->
```
> []($block.attrs('code-tabs'))
> **iroh**
> ```ts
> import { init } from "@pancake/web";
>
> const web = await init({
> 	wasm: fetch("/pancake_host.wasm"),
> 	relayUrl: (locator) => `wss://relay.example/?locator=${encodeURIComponent(locator)}`,
> });
> await using runtime = web.runtime;
> // `@pancake/iroh` loads native FFI, so a page parses the locator on the web runtime.
> const partner = web.parseLocator("ocapn://<64 lowercase hex characters>.iroh");
> ```
> **webrtc**
> ```ts
> import { connector, locator, type SignalingAuthority } from "@pancake/webrtc";
> import { init } from "@pancake/web";
>
> declare const signaling: SignalingAuthority;
> const web = await init({
> 	wasm: fetch("/pancake_host.wasm"),
> 	connectors: [connector(signaling)],
> });
> await using runtime = web.runtime;
> const partner = locator("<64 lowercase hex characters from the peer>");
> ```
> **keyed-tcp**
> ```ts
> import { keyed } from "@pancake/tcp";
> import { init } from "@pancake/web";
>
> const web = await init({
> 	wasm: fetch("/pancake_host.wasm"),
> 	// A page cannot open raw TCP, so a relay carries the bytes.
> 	wsUrl: (host, port) => `ws://127.0.0.1:9000/?to=${host}:${port}`,
> });
> await using runtime = web.runtime;
> // keyed-tcp is cleartext through the relay. Use WebRTC for a sealed browser path.
> const partner = keyed.locator("<64 lowercase hex characters>", "127.0.0.1", 9099);
> ```
> **keyed-relay**
> ```ts
> import { connector, keyed } from "@pancake/relay";
> import { init } from "@pancake/web";
>
> const relayUrl = "wss://relay.example/rendezvous";
> const web = await init({
> 	wasm: fetch("/pancake_host.wasm"),
> 	connectors: [connector()],
> });
> await using runtime = web.runtime;
> const partner = keyed.locator("<64 lowercase hex characters>", relayUrl);
> ```
> **tcp**
> ```ts
> import { locator } from "@pancake/tcp";
> import { init } from "@pancake/web";
>
> const web = await init({
> 	wasm: fetch("/pancake_host.wasm"),
> 	// A page cannot open raw TCP, so a relay carries the bytes.
> 	wsUrl: (host, port) => `ws://127.0.0.1:9000/?to=${host}:${port}`,
> });
> await using runtime = web.runtime;
> const partner = locator("127.0.0.1", 9099);
> ```

From here, the CapTP workflow is the same for every transport:

```ts
await using session = await runtime.connect(partner);
const greeter = await session.fetch("greeter"); // a capability this peer published
const reply = await session.send(greeter, "greet", ["world"]);
console.log(reply);
```
```=html
<!-- /site-examples:dial:browser -->
```

When you hold the key and address as separate pieces instead, import
`@pancake/tcp` before `init` and use `tcp.keyed.locator`. The locator package
does not load native FFI or depend on the initialized wasm adapter.

`init` instantiates `pancake_host.wasm` (built by `zig build wasm-driver`) and
hands back the configured browser runtime. From there `connect`, `fetch`,
`send`, `spawn`, and `publish` work exactly as on the
[TypeScript page]($link.page('bindings/ts')). The one browser-specific part is how
a dial reaches its peer, below.

## Browser dial paths

A web page binds no socket, so the browser always dials. It reaches other peers
through the connectors installed on its managed Runtime.

To reach a **native** peer, the page dials over WebSocket. A host-and-port dial
opens `ws://host:port/` by default, straight to a peer that accepts WebSocket
itself. For a peer that only speaks raw TCP or iroh, dial through a
[relay]($link.page('relay')): `init`'s `wsUrl` option maps a host-and-port dial
to the relay's URL, and `relayUrl` does the same for a native-only locator such
as iroh. The relay never parses a frame, so the keyed handshake and every
capability run end to end between the two peers.

To reach **another browser**, open a direct
[WebRTC]($link.page('transports/webrtc')) DataChannel. Once the channel is up, no
server sits on the byte path. Two browser peers that first met through a relay can also
cold-dial each other by identity, which `init`'s `rendezvousUrl` enables.

## Next

The [TypeScript page]($link.page('bindings/ts')) is the full API, and the
browser uses every verb of it except `listen`. For how those bytes get carried or
bridged, read [the relay]($link.page('relay')) and
[WebRTC]($link.page('transports/webrtc')). The
[`@pancake/web` README](https://github.com/jimisaacs/pancake/blob/main/bindings/ts/web/README.md)
covers installing the package and serving the wasm.
